# Copy to `.env` next to docker-compose.yml and edit.
#
# **None of this is required.** `docker compose up -d` on its own brings up a
# working bastion: the server generates its own signing key, prints a generated
# administrator password to the log, and the console then walks you through
# setup before it lets you register a cluster.
#
# This file is for deciding those things up front instead — which is what you
# want when the install is scripted, when secrets come out of a manager, or when
# several replicas of the image have to agree on a signing key. Anything set
# here wins over what setup would have asked for.

# --- credentials --------------------------------------------------------------

# The database password. Nothing outside this compose file reaches Postgres —
# it publishes no port — but the value still ends up in two places, so generate
# one rather than leaving the default:
#   openssl rand -base64 24
# DB_PASSWORD=

# Signs every session token the console and generated kubeconfigs carry. Left
# unset, the server mints one on first boot and keeps it in the database, so
# sessions survive a restart; set it when the key has to come from somewhere
# else or be shared between replicas. Changing it invalidates every issued
# token, which is also how you revoke them all at once.
#   openssl rand -base64 48
# JWT_SECRET=

# The first administrator, created on first boot only. With no password set one
# is generated and printed once to the log — `docker compose logs kubemg`.
# Changing either later here does nothing; change the password in the console.
# KUBEMG_ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
# KUBEMG_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# The address *target clusters* and operators reach this host on. It is baked
# into every rendered agent manifest and every generated kubeconfig, so
# `localhost` produces an agent that dials itself and never connects. Use the
# LAN/VPN address or DNS name, with the port. This is the one value setup will
# not let you past without, so leaving it here is only a question of whether you
# would rather answer it now or in the browser.
# KUBEMG_PUBLIC_URL=https://kubemg.internal:8443

# --- TLS ----------------------------------------------------------------------

# Every hostname and IP the certificate must be valid for, beyond the public
# URL's host and loopback. A cluster dialling a name that is not listed here
# fails the handshake.
# KUBEMG_TLS_HOSTS=kubemg.internal,192.0.2.10

# A real certificate does not need a variable at all: put tls.crt and tls.key in
# the `ssl` directory next to docker-compose.yml — certbot's fullchain.pem and
# privkey.pem are recognised there too — and it is served on the next restart, in
# place of the self-signed one. See ssl/README.md.
#
# This variable is the stricter version of the same decision. Set it to false and
# the server refuses to start without a real certificate rather than minting one,
# which is what you want where a self-signed certificate silently pinned into
# every agent package would be worse than a failed boot.
# KUBEMG_TLS_SELF_SIGNED=true

# Where that certificate is looked for, if `ssl` next to the compose file is not
# where you keep it. It is a path *inside the container*, so changing it means
# changing the mount above to match.
# KUBEMG_TLS_SUPPLIED_DIR=/etc/kubemg/ssl

# Behind a proxy that terminates TLS, this container can serve plaintext — but
# only deliberately. Its port is published, so a plaintext listener reachable
# from more than the host is refused at boot rather than warned about: every
# session token would transit unencrypted and be replayable. Both of these are
# needed to run that way, and the second matters as much as the first — agents
# verify the *proxy's* certificate, not one this process can see, so nothing
# here can infer the chain they must trust.
# KUBEMG_TLS_ENABLED=false
# KUBEMG_ALLOW_INSECURE=true
# KUBEMG_AGENT_CA_BUNDLE=/etc/kubemg/ssl/proxy-ca.crt

# --- air-gapped installs ------------------------------------------------------

# Point these at an internal mirror and nothing is pulled from the internet.
# The agent image is the one *your clusters* pull, not this host — so it has to
# be reachable from them.
# KUBEMG_IMAGE=registry.internal/kubemg/kubemg:0.8.0
# KUBEMG_POSTGRES_IMAGE=registry.internal/postgres:16-alpine
# KUBEMG_AGENT_IMAGE=registry.internal/kubemg/kubemg-agent:0.8.0

# --- optional -----------------------------------------------------------------

# Encrypts terminal session recordings at rest. 32 bytes, hex or base64:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# Unset, recordings are written in plaintext and the server says so at boot.
# Keep this somewhere other than this host: a key stored beside the ciphertext
# protects nobody.
# KUBEMG_SESSION_RECORDING_KEY=

# Where the host publishes the console and gateway.
# KUBEMG_PORT=8443

# The namespace agents are installed into on target clusters.
# KUBEMG_AGENT_NAMESPACE=kubemg-system
